/// \defgroup grp_w2_string_encoding Quoted-printable and UTF-8 string encoding
/// \ingroup grp_common_services
/// \brief The engine's small text-encoding helpers: the underscore-escape
///        codec that stores arbitrary text in preference and map-cache
///        files, the UTF-8 decoding behind UnicodeString::translate, and a
///        reader for NUL-terminated UTF-16 strings in a file.
///
/// \par The underscore escape ("quoted-printable")
/// The four QuotedPrintable functions are not MIME quoted-printable. Every
/// byte that is not an ASCII letter or digit (as isalnum judges it) becomes
/// an underscore followed by two upper-case hex digits; letters and digits
/// pass through unchanged. The encoded text therefore holds only letters,
/// digits and underscores, so it survives as one token in INI files and in
/// the key = value preference files, whatever spaces, equals signs, commas or
/// non-ASCII characters the original held. Examples, worked from the bodies:
/// - AsciiStringToQuotedPrintable turns "My Map.map" into "My_20Map_2Emap".
/// - UnicodeStringToQuotedPrintable escapes the raw little-endian UTF-16
///   bytes, so L"Ab c" becomes "A_00b_00_20_00c_00": every ASCII character
///   carries a "_00" for its zero high byte.
/// - A literal underscore is written as "_5F", which is what makes the
///   escape reversible.
///
/// The decoders reverse this. Each of the four functions works in a
/// function-local static buffer of 1,024 characters and returns a copy, so
/// the buffer is reused by the next call. Encoders stop adding characters
/// near the end of that buffer; the decoders check no length at all (see
/// each function).
///
/// \par Who uses it in BFME 2
/// From matched callers:
/// - Preference files. SkirmishPreferences encodes the player-name list,
///   the current user name and per-user keys, and its constructor decodes
///   the name list. The LAN lobby's user-name setter stores the LAN name
///   encoded (its ledger name, LanLobbyUserNamePrefs::setUserName, is a
///   descriptive label carried over from BFME 1; Zero Hour does the same
///   store through LANPreferences in LanLobbyMenu.cpp).
///   LadderPreferences::write and LadderPreferences::loadProfile
///   encode and decode each ladder's address and name.
///   GameSpyLoginPreferences::write stores each remembered password (after
///   obfuscate) and date encoded, and GameSpyLoginPreferences::load decodes
///   them. QuickMatchPreferences::setMapSelected and
///   QuickMatchPreferences::isMapSelected use the encoded map name as the
///   key. A mode-keyed preferences class, not yet named (the reconstruction
///   labels it GameModePreferences descriptively), stores its map, game
///   name and password settings encoded, and decodes those and its stored
///   user name when reading them back. Its accessor names are not yet
///   recovered.
/// - The map cache. MapCache::writeCacheINI writes each map's name encoded
///   on its MapCache header line and its display name and description with
///   the Unicode encoder; INI::parseMapCacheDefinition decodes all three
///   when the cache is read back.
///
/// Zero Hour also sends GameSpy chat text through
/// UnicodeStringToQuotedPrintable and QuotedPrintableToUnicodeString, and
/// its LANAPI stores the host player's name encoded beside a remembered
/// direct-connect address in the LAN preferences. Neither use has been
/// traced in BFME 2 yet.
///
/// \par UTF-8 in UnicodeString
/// UnicodeString::translate(const char *) decodes UTF-8 through Windows'
/// MultiByteToWideChar. UnicodeString's constructor from an AsciiString and
/// UnicodeString::translate(const AsciiString &) both forward to it, so
/// those UnicodeString members read narrow input as UTF-8. Zero Hour's
/// UnicodeString::translate instead widened each byte on its own, which is
/// correct only for 7-bit ASCII. The reverse direction is
/// AsciiString::translate, which encodes UTF-8. Not every narrow-to-wide
/// conversion in BFME 2 is UTF-8: the engine also has a separate wrapper
/// that converts with the system ANSI code page (code page 0; its ledger
/// name, multiByteToWideChar, is descriptive), and some code uses it to
/// fill a UnicodeString's buffer directly.
///
/// \par Source layout
/// Zero Hour had one QuotedPrintable.cpp. In BFME 2 retail the four
/// conversion bodies are built with an exception-handling frame, which the
/// unit holding the two hex-digit helpers is not, so the reconstruction
/// moves them out of quoted_printable.cpp into
/// quoted_printable_encoders.cpp, quoted_printable_ascii.cpp and
/// quoted_printable_unicode.cpp, all under WWVegas/WWLib and all compiled
/// with the same settings. The ledger keeps the two hex-digit helpers in
/// quoted_printable.cpp. Retail holds one out-of-line copy of each helper,
/// shared by the encoders or by the decoders; each split unit defines its
/// own file-static copy, which compiles to that same body.
///
/// BFME 2's own Code/ tree has no header for these functions. Most calling
/// units declare the four prototypes locally. Some units include Zero
/// Hour's Common/QuotedPrintable.h through the reference include path, and
/// quoted_printable.cpp includes BFME 1's quoted_printable.h, which has the
/// same four prototypes. UnicodeString::translate(const char *) is in
/// StringUtf8Translation.cpp, and readUnicodeString in
/// GameEngine/Source/Common/System/ReadUnicodeString.cpp.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - None of the matched callers of the four quoted-printable functions is
///   game-logic code. They read and write local preference files and the
///   map cache, so as far as traced the escape does not touch lockstep, the
///   logic random-number generator or the CRC checks. Changing the escape
///   does break reading files written by the original game. In Zero Hour
///   the escape also travels over the network in chat, where it is part of
///   the format other clients expect.
/// - readUnicodeString is a different case. If its caller is the replay
///   reader, as inferred (see the function), changing how it reads strings
///   breaks reading existing replay files.
/// - When editing an encoded value in a preference file or the map cache
///   by hand, letters and digits can be written as they are and an
///   underscore must be written "_5F". Escape every other byte the same
///   way, as the game does, so that the value stays one token. In a field
///   that the game decodes as Unicode, each character needs both of its
///   UTF-16 bytes ("A" is "A_00").
/// - Narrow text passed through UnicodeString::translate (for example LAN
///   host and login names received from other machines) is decoded as
///   UTF-8, so non-UTF-8 bytes from a peer may not display as intended.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// - All four quoted-printable functions use fixed static buffers. They
///   are not reentrant and not thread-safe, and long input is truncated
///   (encoders) or overruns the buffer (decoders, and in one edge case the
///   Unicode encoder). A port should size the output from the input.
/// - The Unicode encoder escapes UTF-16 little-endian bytes. A port whose
///   wide character is 32 bits wide, or big-endian, must still produce and
///   read UTF-16LE bytes to stay compatible with existing preference files
///   and map caches.
/// - The UTF-8 decoding is a thin call to the Win32 MultiByteToWideChar
///   with code page 65001 and no flags; that call is the seam to replace.
///   UnicodeString holds 16-bit code units, so characters outside the
///   Basic Multilingual Plane become surrogate pairs.
/// - readUnicodeString reads through the C runtime's fgetwc, whose result
///   depends on the runtime and on the mode the file was opened in; where
///   wchar_t is 32 bits wide it decodes by locale. A port should read raw
///   16-bit little-endian units instead.

/// \file Common/QuotedPrintable.h
/// \ingroup grp_w2_string_encoding
/// \brief Zero Hour's declarations of the four quoted-printable functions.
///
/// BFME 2's own Code/ tree has no header for these functions. Most calling
/// units declare the four prototypes locally. Some units include this Zero
/// Hour copy through the reference include path, and quoted_printable.cpp
/// includes BFME 1's quoted_printable.h, which has the same four
/// prototypes. The Zero Hour copy is listed here so that the declarations
/// can be documented; its prototypes match the BFME 2 definitions.

/// \file quoted_printable_encoders.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w2_string_encoding
/// \brief The two quoted-printable encoders, UnicodeStringToQuotedPrintable
///        and AsciiStringToQuotedPrintable, with a file-static intToHexDigit.
///
/// Bodies adapted from Zero Hour's QuotedPrintable.cpp. Unlike the decoders,
/// the encoders keep Zero Hour's output cap, and the Unicode encoder keeps
/// its loop that handles two bytes per pass.

/// \file quoted_printable_ascii.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w2_string_encoding
/// \brief QuotedPrintableToAsciiString, with a file-static hexDigitToInt.
///
/// Zero Hour's body without its length counter, which Zero Hour declared
/// but never advanced. The Unicode decoder lives in
/// quoted_printable_unicode.cpp and has the same shape.

/// \file ReadUnicodeString.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w2_string_encoding
/// \brief readUnicodeString: reads one NUL-terminated UTF-16 string from a
///        C runtime FILE.
///
/// Ported from Open-BFME-1's ReadUnicodeString.cpp. Retail keeps no symbol
/// for this function; the name is carried from the BFME 1 donor.

/// \fn AsciiString AsciiStringToQuotedPrintable(AsciiString original)
/// \brief Escapes a narrow string so that it contains only letters, digits
///        and underscores.
///
/// Callers use it to store a map name, an obfuscated password, a date or an
/// address as one token in a preference file or the map-cache INI: see the
/// group description for the callers. QuotedPrintableToAsciiString reverses
/// it.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Each byte for which isalnum is true is copied. Every other byte becomes
/// an underscore and the two upper-case hex digits of the byte, high nibble
/// first, from intToHexDigit. Output goes to a function-local static buffer
/// of 1,024 characters, and a new byte is started only while fewer than
/// 1,021 characters have been written. The longest output is therefore
/// 1,023 characters, and longer input is cut silently: about 341 characters
/// if every one needs escaping.
///
/// \param original String to encode, taken by value. An empty string gives
///                 an empty result.
/// \return The encoded copy.
///
/// \note The byte reaches isalnum as a plain char, so bytes from 0x80 up are
///       passed as negative values, which the C standard leaves undefined.
///       Which of them are escaped depends on the C runtime and locale.
///       The escape for such a byte is still correct when it happens: the
///       high nibble is taken from the unsigned byte.
/// \see QuotedPrintableToAsciiString, UnicodeStringToQuotedPrintable,
///      intToHexDigit

/// \fn AsciiString UnicodeStringToQuotedPrintable(UnicodeString original)
/// \brief Escapes the raw UTF-16 bytes of a wide string into a narrow string
///        of letters, digits and underscores.
///
/// This is how wide text such as player names, ladder names and map display
/// names is stored in narrow preference files and the map-cache INI:
/// SkirmishPreferences, the LAN lobby's user-name setter (see the group
/// description on its name), LadderPreferences::write and
/// MapCache::writeCacheINI call it.
/// QuotedPrintableToUnicodeString reverses it.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// The string's characters are read as a byte array in memory order, which
/// on x86 is little-endian: low byte first. Each byte is copied if isalnum
/// accepts it and otherwise becomes an underscore and two upper-case hex
/// digits, as in AsciiStringToQuotedPrintable. The loop handles one whole
/// character (two bytes) per pass and stops at a character whose two bytes
/// are both zero. Each character costs two to six output characters; an
/// ASCII letter or digit costs four ("A_00").
///
/// \param original String to encode, taken by value.
/// \return The encoded copy.
///
/// \warning The cap on the 1,024-character static buffer is tested only once
///          per character, before up to six characters are written. Input
///          that reaches the cap can write up to three bytes past the end of
///          the buffer, including the terminator. Zero Hour's loop has the
///          same shape.
/// \note BFME 2's body takes the high nibble from the unsigned byte. Zero
///       Hour's source shifts the signed char, so a byte from 0x80 up gives
///       a negative nibble, which intToHexDigit turns into a NUL that ends
///       the encoded string early.
/// \see QuotedPrintableToUnicodeString, AsciiStringToQuotedPrintable

/// \fn AsciiString QuotedPrintableToAsciiString(AsciiString original)
/// \brief Decodes a string produced by AsciiStringToQuotedPrintable back to
///        its original bytes.
///
/// Called when preference values and the map cache are read back. Callers
/// include INI::parseMapCacheDefinition for the map name,
/// GameSpyLoginPreferences::load for remembered passwords and dates, and
/// LadderPreferences::loadProfile for ladder addresses; the group
/// description lists other matched callers.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Characters other than the underscore are copied. An underscore takes the
/// next character as a hex digit and, if one more character follows, a
/// second digit, and the result is one byte. Digits go through
/// hexDigitToInt, so a character that is not a hex digit counts as zero,
/// and an underscore at the very end is dropped. Output goes to a
/// function-local static buffer of 1,024 bytes and is then copied into the
/// returned string, which ends at the first zero byte: a decoded "_00"
/// truncates the result.
///
/// \param original Encoded string, taken by value. Any text is accepted;
///                 nothing is rejected as malformed.
/// \return The decoded string.
///
/// \warning No length is checked. Input that decodes to more than 1,023
///          bytes writes past the static buffer. Output from
///          AsciiStringToQuotedPrintable is always short enough, but a
///          hand-edited or corrupted file need not be.
/// \note Zero Hour's version declares a length counter that it never
///       advances, so it is unbounded too. BFME 2's compiled body has no
///       counter at all.
/// \see AsciiStringToQuotedPrintable, QuotedPrintableToUnicodeString,
///      hexDigitToInt

/// \fn UnicodeString QuotedPrintableToUnicodeString(AsciiString original)
/// \brief Decodes a string produced by UnicodeStringToQuotedPrintable back to
///        a wide string.
///
/// Callers include SkirmishPreferences's constructor for the stored
/// player-name list, INI::parseMapCacheDefinition for map display names and
/// descriptions, and LadderPreferences::loadProfile for ladder names; the
/// group description lists other matched callers.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// The escape is undone exactly as in QuotedPrintableToAsciiString, but the
/// resulting bytes are written into a static buffer of 1,024 UTF-16
/// characters in memory order, so each pair of bytes forms one
/// little-endian character. At the end, an even byte count gets a whole
/// zero character as terminator. For an odd count, only the high byte of
/// the last character is cleared and no terminator is written, so the
/// string then ends at the next zero character already in the buffer,
/// which may be text left by an earlier, longer call. The encoder always
/// writes whole characters, so this only happens with damaged input.
///
/// \param original Encoded string, taken by value.
/// \return The decoded wide string.
///
/// \warning No length is checked. Input that decodes to more than 1,023
///          characters writes past the static buffer.
/// \see UnicodeStringToQuotedPrintable, QuotedPrintableToAsciiString,
///      hexDigitToInt

/// \fn char intToHexDigit(int num)
/// \brief Returns the upper-case hex digit for a value from 0 to 15.
///
/// The encoders' nibble-to-character step.
/// \param num Value to convert.
/// \return '0' to '9' or 'A' to 'F'; NUL if num is outside 0 to 15.
/// \see hexDigitToInt, AsciiStringToQuotedPrintable,
///      UnicodeStringToQuotedPrintable

/// \fn int hexDigitToInt(char c)
/// \brief Returns the value of a hex digit, accepting either case.
///
/// The decoders' character-to-nibble step.
/// \param c Character to convert.
/// \return 0 to 15 for '0' to '9', 'a' to 'f' or 'A' to 'F'. Any other
///         character gives 0; there is no error value.
/// \see intToHexDigit, QuotedPrintableToAsciiString,
///      QuotedPrintableToUnicodeString

/// \fn void UnicodeString::translate(const char *that)
/// \ingroup grp_w2_string_encoding
/// \brief Replaces this string's contents with UTF-8 text decoded to UTF-16.
///
/// This is UnicodeString's own narrow-to-wide conversion. UnicodeString's
/// constructor from an AsciiString and UnicodeString::translate(const
/// AsciiString &) forward to it, and so do GameTextManager::fetchPtr (in
/// the mode where it returns the label itself instead of a looked-up
/// string), UserPreferences::load (for the preference file's name) and the
/// LANAPI message handlers that store a LAN player's host and login names.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Two calls to a wrapper around MultiByteToWideChar with code page 65001
/// (UTF-8) and no flags: the first, with no output buffer, returns the
/// number of UTF-16 characters needed including the terminator; the string
/// then gets an unshared buffer of that size, without keeping its old
/// contents, and the second call decodes into it. On success the stored
/// length is the decoded count without the terminator. A null or empty
/// input, or a failed conversion, releases the buffer and leaves the
/// string empty.
///
/// \param that NUL-terminated UTF-8 text, or null.
///
/// \note Zero Hour has only UnicodeString::translate(const AsciiString &),
///       which widens each byte separately and so is right only for 7-bit
///       ASCII. With no flags, Windows does not fail on invalid UTF-8; how
///       it maps invalid bytes depends on the Windows version.
/// \see AsciiString::translate, UnicodeString::translate(const AsciiString &)

/// \fn UnicodeString readUnicodeString(_iobuf *file)
/// \brief Reads one NUL-terminated string of 16-bit characters from a C
///        runtime file.
///
/// _iobuf is the Microsoft C runtime's name for the structure behind FILE.
/// In BFME 2 the only caller found so far is a small member function, not
/// yet named, that passes its object's FILE pointer through. That is the
/// shape of the readUnicodeString member of BFME 1's RecorderClass, which
/// reads the replay name and version strings of a replay header this way;
/// the BFME 2 caller's identity is not yet confirmed.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Characters are read one at a time with fgetwc into a local buffer of
/// 1,024 characters until a zero character, WEOF (0xFFFF) or the limit.
/// The zero character is consumed from the file. At most 1,023 characters
/// are kept; an overlong string is cut there, one character past the cut
/// has already been read and is dropped, and the file is left inside the
/// string.
///
/// \param file Open file positioned at the start of the string. Not
///             checked for null.
/// \return The string read, possibly empty.
///
/// \note Only characters after the first are compared with WEOF. The check
///       on the first character compares with EOF (-1), which a 16-bit
///       result never equals, so at end of file the function returns a
///       one-character string holding U+FFFF instead of an empty one.
/// \note Zero Hour's RecorderClass has its own copy of this reader as a
///       member. It compares every character with EOF and bounds its loop
///       at 1,024. With the Microsoft runtime, whose fgetwc returns an
///       unsigned 16-bit value, it therefore keeps reading at end of file
///       until that limit, and its last store falls one element past its
///       buffer. The BFME 1 and BFME 2 body stops at WEOF and at 1,023.
